findings.body:
The exported class was renamed, but nothing that uses it was updated:

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="5-5"}

These files still import `SelectedCalendarRepository` from `@calcom/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository`:

- ::file{file="apps/web/app/api/cron/calendar-subscriptions/route.ts"} (lines 11 and 41, `new SelectedCalendarRepository(prisma)`)
- ::file{file="apps/web/app/api/webhooks/calendar-subscription/[provider]/route.ts"} (lines 14 and 61)
- ::file{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.test.ts"} (lines 3 and 53)

::callout{type="warning"}
The import resolves to `undefined`, so TypeScript fails the build. If it somehow ships, `new SelectedCalendarRepository(prisma)` throws `TypeError: ... is not a constructor` on every cron run and on every incoming calendar webhook. The repository test file breaks the same way, so none of the new tests actually run.

The rename isn't mentioned in the PR description and doesn't relate to the feature. The simplest fix is to revert it:

::suggestion{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="5-5"}
export class SelectedCalendarRepository implements ISelectedCalendarRepository {

Google gives `@group.calendar.google.com` IDs to every non-primary calendar a user creates, such as a "Work" or "Side project" calendar, as well as to calendars shared with the user. These are often the personal calendars people pick for conflict checking, which is what the cache is meant to speed up.

::diff{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="12-20"}

With this suffix in the list, those calendars are never subscribed and never cached. Availability for them falls back to live fetches without any signal. Holidays and contacts use `@group.v.calendar.google.com`, while import and resource calendars have their own suffixes. Please confirm that excluding all `@group.calendar.google.com` calendars is intended. If it isn't, drop this entry or explain in the code comment why these calendars must be skipped (for example, if watch channels can't be created on them).

Inside the truthy branch, `genericCalendarSuffixes` is already known to be non-empty, so the second `?.` does nothing:

::diff{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="41-45"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/selectedCalendar/repositories/SelectedCalendarRepository.ts" lines="41-45"}
        AND: genericCalendarSuffixes?.length
          ? genericCalendarSuffixes.map((suffix) => ({
              NOT: { externalId: { endsWith: suffix } },
            }))
          : undefined,

Separately, `getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` flattens the suffixes for *all* providers, including ones `getProviders()` doesn't currently return. This is harmless today, but `Object.values(GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES).flat()` would be simpler than `Object.keys` plus a cast. Deriving the list from `getProviders()` would keep the two methods consistent.

The service tests mock `getGenericCalendarSuffixes`, so the real flattening logic in `DefaultAdapterFactory` is never exercised. Both the service tests and the actual constant hard-code the same four strings, which means changing the constant wouldn't break any test.

::diff{file="packages/features/calendar-subscription/adapters/AdaptersFactory.ts" lines="61-71"}

A small test that calls `new DefaultAdapterFactory().getGenericCalendarSuffixes()` and compares the result to `GENERIC_CALENDAR_SUFFIXES` would cover it. The service test could also import the constant instead of repeating the literals.
